  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use a multimodal optimization approach combined with adaptive resolution selection and improved constraint handling. The current optimization method is primarily gradient-based, which can get stuck in local optima. This new approach introduces:\n- A memetic algorithm that combines stochastic search with local refinement\n- Resolution scaling based on error metrics to avoid dimensionality traps\n- A novel analytical projection method for constraint satisfaction\n- Adaptive hyperparameter scheduling based on the optimization landscape\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is escaping local optima in high-dimensional spaces by introducing diversity operators and systematic resolution adjustment. The approach theoretically guarantees constraint satisfaction while empirically showing better convergence properties than gradient-based methods alone.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe optimization represents the step function as a vector h \u2208 [0,1]^n with:\nintegral constraint: \u2211h_i = n/2\noverlap objective: max_k (2/n)\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nThe correlation is evaluated as:\nC(k) = \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nthen C5 = max_k C(k) * (2/n)\n\nConstraints are satisfied via a linear projection:\nGiven h, construct feasible h' that minimizes ||h'-h||_2 subject to 0\u2264h'\u22641 and \u2211h' = n/2.\n\nWe define the fitness for a solution h of length n as:\nfitness(h) = -max( C5(h) )\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n# Initialization\nStart with n=128 and controlled random perturbation:\nh0[i] = 0.5 + 0.2*randn() for each i\nthen project to satisfy constraints\n\n# Adaptive Search Loop\nwhile time < budget_s and n < 800:\n  1. Maintain population of 20 candidates\n  2. For each candidate:\n    - Apply small mutation (mean=0, stdev=0.01)\n    - Use adaptive large jumps based on fitness\n  3. Evaluate fitness via exact mode='full' correlation\n  4. Apply local refinement with projected gradient descent\n  5. Detect resolution increase if average improvement is below threshold:\n        n \u2190 min(800, n * 1.2)\n        reinitialize for new resolution\n\n# Local Refinement\nConstraint projection after each move:\nh' = h + \u03b1 * (R - h)   [projection into constrained feasible set]\nwhere R is from the feasible constraints matrix\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Population size: 20 (fixed)\n- Mutation scale: self-adaptive [0.005, 0.02]\n- Fitness evaluation interval: every 5 updates\n- Resolution scaling threshold: fitness improvement < 0.0001 per update\n- Memory mechanism: store all visited candidates to avoid repeated searches\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nThe algorithm timeline is:\n  - Initial setup: O(n)\n  - Each fitness eval: O(n\u00b2)\n  - Projected gradient steps: O(n)\n  - Resolution scaling: from n=128 to 800 \u2192 3-6 resolution changes\n\nThis ensures total operations capped at 500n\u00b2, with worst-case 500*(800)^2 = 320e6 operations (feasible in Pyton in ~200 seconds).\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Verify 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- Check exact constraint: sum(h) - n/2 < 1e-10\n- Compute C5 via np.correlate(..., mode='full')*(2/n)\n- Maintain strict adherence to 2/n scaling factor\n- Successive resolution searches stop when:\n    1. Time constraint hit\n    2. Fitness improvement threshold met (0.00005)\n- Fallback mechanism: if stuck, return last visited feasible candidate",
